    <statement><p>Find the critical point and determine if the function is increasing or decreasing on the given intervals.</p>
<p><m>y= - x^2 + 6 x  - 3</m></p>
<p>Critical point: <m>c=</m> <fillin characters="4" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p>The function is:</p>
<p><var form="popup">
<li>
<p>?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Decreasing</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Increasing</p>
</li>
</var> on <m>(-\infty,c)</m>.</p>
<p><var form="popup">
<li>
<p>?</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Increasing</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Decreasing</p>
</li>
</var> on <m>(c,\infty)</m>.</p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><alert> Solution:</alert> 



Let <m>f(x)= - x^2 + 6 x  - 3</m>. Then <m>f'(x)= -2x + 6 = 0</m> yields the critical point <m>c=3</m>. 


As shown in the following table, the critical point <m>c=3</m> is a local maximum. 





